## Introduction

To a first-time builder, a construction site can look chaotic — sand here, blocks there, men standing around a half-dug trench. In reality, a well-run build follows a strict sequence, and understanding that sequence is what lets you supervise your own project with confidence instead of anxiety.

This guide follows the process from the first survey peg to the roof sheet, explaining what happens at each stage, what materials and labour are needed, and — critically — what must be finished and inspected before the next stage can safely begin.

## What "Foundation to Roof" Actually Means

The phrase is Nigerian building shorthand for a defined scope: the structural carcass — foundation, blockwork, lintels, decking where designed, and the roof — complete and weather-tight, with services *first-fixed* (conduits and pipe drops in the walls) but nothing finished. No plaster, no screed, no POP, no paint. A build "to roof" is a closed box waiting to become a building.

Why so many Nigerians build to exactly this line is not fashion; it is the honest economics of cash flow. The structural phase is money in the *building* — every naira spent is load-bearing and permanent. Finishing money spent early, out of sequence, buys decoration that weather and workmanship can damage; structure bought right sits and gains nothing by waiting. So the population builds to roof in one programme, then finishes room by room as money returns. This guide is the map of that first programme.

## Stage 1 — Survey, Setting Out, and Excavation

**What happens:** the land is cleared, the building's corners are transferred from drawing to ground via builder's square and profiles (the pegs and boards that hold the string lines), and the trenches are dug to the design depth.

**What you watch:** the diagonals. A rectangle's diagonals must be equal before anyone digs — a 100 mm setting-out error is a boundary dispute or a building that fights its own drawing forever. Trench depth is checked against the design, not against the neighbour's trench; soil type decides depth, and the engineer's figure outranks everyone's confidence.

**The gate to the next stage:** profiles immovable, diagonals equal, trenches to depth and clean of loose spoil at the bottom.

## Stage 2 — Foundation Concrete and Blockwork to DPC

**What happens:** blinding seals the trench bottom, reinforcement (where the design calls for it) is fixed on chairs, then the footing concrete is cast in one continuous push per section. After curing, the block courses rise from the footing to damp-proof-course level.

**What you watch:** the mix — a 1:2:4 concrete batched by head-pan is roughly one pan of cement, two of sand, four of granite, and *no extra water for comfort*; every splash of over-watering is strength leaving the foundation permanently. The DPC membrane then runs continuous across the wall tops before backfill — the unbroken line that stops ground moisture for the life of the building.

**The gate:** concrete cured to schedule (about a week before backfilling and loading, 28 days to design strength), blockwork level and plumb, DPC continuous and unbroken — the cheapest stage to protect from damp and the most expensive one to repair from it.

## Stage 3 — Backfill, Compaction, and the Ground Floor

**What happens:** the excavated soil (or better, selected sharp sand) returns into the building against the DPC-level walls, compacted in layers, then hardcore or filling is blinded and the ground floor slab is cast.

**What you watch:** compaction in layers — a single deep fill settles for years and takes the floor slab's level with it; layer-by-layer ramming is the difference. The fill must not bridge or puncture the DPC: backfilling carelessly against the membrane is how the barrier fails at the one wall nobody re-checks.

**The gate:** fill compacted, levelled, and dry before the slab pour; slab cured before anything stores on it.

## Stage 4 — Superstructure Blockwork

**What happens:** the walls rise course by course above DPC — the external walls in the block size the design specifies, the internal partitions thinner, the openings formed with props and planks where the door/window schedule says they are.

**What you watch:** level and plumb *as the work proceeds* — a four-course error caught at course two is a five-minute correction; caught at lintel height it is a wall. Wall ties at corners, the openings' widths matching the schedule (a lintel ordered for 900 mm does not forgive a 940 mm opening), and the first-fix conduits and pipe drops entering the walls now — services *behind* the blockwork is the access logic at work.

**The gate:** all walls to lintel height, level and plumb, openings true to the schedule, first-fix in and tested where testable.

## Stage 5 — Lintels, Beams, and Decking

**What happens:** reinforced concrete lintels span every opening; where the design has suspended floors, the decking follows — formwork set, reinforcement fixed per the drawings, concrete cast, then the patient part: curing.

**What you watch:** bearings (a lintel sits on real blockwork each side — bearing length is structural, not decoration), the steel schedule as drawn (the drawing's bar sizes and spacings outrank the site's habits), formwork tightness (a gap you can pass a pencil through passes cement slurry onto everything below), and — above all — the curing calendar. Decking loaded early cracks in the soffit; the shoring stays until the concrete has earned its strength. Materials arrive on the deck *after* the calendar, not after impatience.

**The gate:** concrete cured per schedule, shoring struck in the right order, soffit free of honeycombs, deck loading allowed.

## Stage 6 — Roofing

**What happens:** wall plates are bedded level around the top of the wallwork, the timber structure (or trusses) rises, purlins follow, then sheets go down from eave to ridge, and ridges, hips, and flashings close every line water could find.

**What you watch:** pitch as designed — it is weather performance, not taste; laps per the sheet manufacturer's instructions, not per the roofer's speed; and the hose test before anyone celebrates — a bucket of water poured at the ridge tells you more in five minutes than a month of looking will.

**The gate to "finished programme":** the box is closed, the building is weather-tight, the first-fix services are in the walls, and everything below can now be finished in a controlled climate instead of a weather system.

## What To-Roof Deliberately Excludes — and Why the Line Is Where It Is

Everything after roofing is *finishing*, and the sequence holds it back for one reason: finishes need a closed building. Render needs shade from direct sun; screed needs a roof over its curing water; paint needs dry air and stable walls. A programme that finishes before roofing is building in a weather system and paying for it twice.

The line also protects the money: structure completed and inspected is a building with value that waits patiently; finishes bought out of sequence are inventory — consumable, portable, and exposed. The [construction sequence guide](/learn/complete-guide-building-construction-sequence) explains the full order of operations including the finishing phases.

## Inspecting the Programme — the Owner's Six Checks

You do not need a site degree to supervise a build to roof; you need six checks at the right moments:

1. **Diagonals at setting out** — equal, or nothing proceeds.
2. **Mix discipline at foundation** — the ratios as specified, water controlled.
3. **DPC continuity before backfill** — unbroken, protected during filling.
4. **Level and plumb at superstructure** — checked at every few courses, not at the end.
5. **Curing respected at decking** — the calendar beats the schedule, always.
6. **The hose test at the roof** — before the final payment on the roofing line.

Each check is minutes; each protects a stage whose failure is found in years. That asymmetry — cheap to check, expensive to discover — is the whole logic of supervision, and the [quality control inspection checklist](/learn/construction-quality-control-inspection-checklist) extends it into a full room-by-room protocol for the finishing phases that follow.


## The Site Book — Making the Gates Real

The gates in this guide only work if they are written down. The professional habit is a site book — a plain exercise book that lives on site and records, per stage: the date each gate passed, who checked, and a photograph reference (setting-out diagonals, DPC before backfill, steel before the pour, the hose test at the ridge).

The book earns its keep three times over: it keeps the gates from being declared rather than demonstrated ("cured" becomes a date, not an opinion); it settles every later dispute about what lies inside a wall, under a slab, or behind a membrane; and it becomes the project's memory for the finishing phases — the conduit routes, the pipe drops, the DPC line — which is precisely the information the finishes team needs and nobody can see anymore. Five minutes per gate, written the day it happens, is the cheapest insurance in the entire programme.

## The Money Shape of a To-Roof Programme

The cost profile is a series of peaks, not a slope: foundation and blockwork spike early (concrete, blocks, and the labour of the lift), decking is the single biggest peak (formwork, steel, and a big continuous pour), roofing peaks with timber and sheets, and between the peaks the spend flattens to labour-and-cement lines. The [build-to-roof estimator](/build-to-roof-estimator) prices the whole programme phase by phase from your drawing dimensions, so the peaks become a savings plan instead of surprises; the [materials estimation guide](/learn/how-to-estimate-materials-each-construction-phase) covers the quantity logic behind each line.

Build to roof is also the natural inspection milestone for lenders and family contributions alike — a closed, weather-tight structure is the point at which the project becomes auditable: countable courses, measurable areas, a roofline. Many builders photograph each stage's gate (the diagonals, the DPC, the steel before the pour, the hose test) — a five-minute habit that settles every later question about what lies inside a wall.

## Closing

Foundation to roof is one honest programme: set out square, found on a controlled mix, damp-proofed before filling, risen level, spanned and cured without shortcuts, closed with a tested roof. The stages pass their gates, the money follows the gates, and what remains after the roof is the patient, controllable work of finishing. Start the quantities with the [build-to-roof estimator](/build-to-roof-estimator), and plan the whole programme with [how to plan your build-to-roof project](/learn/how-to-plan-your-build-to-roof-project).
